Terraform Interview Notes — Your Practice
1. What is Terraform?

Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp.

It allows us to define infrastructure using configuration files and create/manage resources such as:

EC2
VPC
Subnets
Security Groups
Load Balancers
S3
IAM
RDS
CloudFront
Route 53

Terraform uses a declarative approach.

Declarative means

We tell Terraform what we want, not every step required to create it.

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

Terraform determines how to create it.

2. Terraform Architecture

Basic flow:

Terraform Configuration
        ↓
Terraform CLI
        ↓
AWS Provider
        ↓
AWS API
        ↓
AWS Infrastructure

Terraform has three important components:

Configuration

.tf files define desired infrastructure.

Provider

Connects Terraform to AWS.

provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}
State

Terraform records infrastructure information in:

terraform.tfstate
3. Terraform Files

Common Terraform files:

File	Purpose
main.tf	Main resources
variables.tf	Variable declarations
terraform.tfvars	Variable values
outputs.tf	Output values
providers.tf	Provider configuration
versions.tf	Terraform/provider requirements
.terraform.lock.hcl	Locks provider versions
terraform.tfstate	Infrastructure state
4. Terraform Installation

Check Terraform:

terraform version

You practiced with:

Terraform v1.16.4
windows_amd64

If Terraform isn't in PATH, you can execute it using its full path, for example:

C:\terraform\terraform.exe version
5. Terraform Provider

Provider tells Terraform which platform/API to communicate with.

Example AWS:

provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

Initialize provider:

terraform init
6. Terraform init
terraform init
Purpose
Initializes Terraform working directory
Downloads providers
Initializes modules
Creates .terraform
Creates/updates .terraform.lock.hcl

Typical flow:

terraform init
      ↓
Download AWS provider
      ↓
Initialize modules
      ↓
Ready for plan/apply
7. Terraform plan
terraform plan

Shows what Terraform plans to change.

Possible actions:

+ create
~ update
- destroy
-/+ replace
Important interview point

terraform plan does not create infrastructure.

It only shows the proposed changes.

8. Terraform apply
terraform apply

Creates/updates infrastructure according to the plan.

Usually:

terraform plan
        ↓
Review
        ↓
terraform apply
9. Terraform destroy
terraform destroy

Destroys resources managed by Terraform.

⚠️ Always review the destruction plan carefully.

10. Terraform Format
terraform fmt

Formats Terraform files according to Terraform's standard formatting.

11. Terraform Validate
terraform validate

Checks whether the Terraform configuration is syntactically and structurally valid.

Example:

Success! The configuration is valid.
12. Variables

Instead of hardcoding values:

instance_type = "t2.micro"

we can use variables:

instance_type = var.instance_type

Declare:

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

Set value in:

instance_type = "t2.micro"

inside terraform.tfvars.

Flow
variables.tf
     ↓
terraform.tfvars
     ↓
var.instance_type
     ↓
resource
13. Variable Types

Common types:

string
number
bool
list
map
set
object

Example:

variable "instance_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "t2.micro"
}
14. Terraform Outputs

Outputs expose useful information after deployment.

Example:

output "instance_id" {
  value = aws_instance.web.id
}

Run:

terraform output
15. Resource

Resource defines infrastructure.

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}

Format:

resource "<provider>_<resource>" "<local_name>"

Example:

aws_instance.web
16. Resource Arguments vs Attributes

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

ami and instance_type are arguments.

After creation, things like:

aws_instance.web.id
aws_instance.web.public_ip

are attributes.

17. References

Terraform resources can reference other resources.

Example:

subnet_id = aws_subnet.public.id

Terraform automatically understands the dependency.

18. Dependency

Terraform creates resources in the required order when references establish dependencies.

Example:

VPC
 ↓
Subnet
 ↓
EC2

Terraform understands:

subnet_id = aws_subnet.public.id

and creates the subnet before the EC2.

19. Explicit Dependency

Sometimes we use:

depends_on = [
  aws_instance.example
]

Use depends_on when Terraform cannot automatically determine the dependency.

20. Terraform State

The default state file is:

terraform.tfstate

It records Terraform's knowledge of infrastructure.

Think:

.tf files
   ↓
Desired configuration

terraform.tfstate
   ↓
Terraform's record of managed infrastructure
View resources
terraform state list
View specific resource
terraform state show aws_instance.example
21. Why State Is Important

Terraform uses state to determine:

What exists?
What changed?
What needs to be created?
What needs to be updated?
What needs to be destroyed?

Without proper state management, Terraform cannot reliably manage infrastructure.

22. Never Commit Local State to Git

Don't push:

terraform.tfstate
terraform.tfstate.*
.terraform/

Your .gitignore should contain:

.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

For team environments, state should generally be stored remotely.

23. Terraform Modules

You practiced modules extensively.

A module is a reusable collection of Terraform configuration.

Example:

project/
│
├── main.tf
│
└── module/
    └── ec2_instance/
        ├── main.tf
        ├── variables.tf
        └── outputs.tf

Call the module:

module "ec2_instance" {
  source = "./module/ec2_instance"

  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  instance_name = var.instance_name
}
24. Why Modules?

Modules provide:

Reusability
Cleaner code
Standardization
Easier maintenance
Separation of infrastructure components

Interview answer:

A module is a reusable and encapsulated collection of Terraform resources that helps us avoid duplicating infrastructure code.

25. Module Variables

Parent:

module "ec2_instance" {
  source = "./module/ec2_instance"

  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  instance_name = var.instance_name
}

Child module:

variable "ami" {
  type = string
}

The argument name passed by the parent must match the variable declared by the child module.

