27. Multiple AWS Regions

You practiced deploying resources in different AWS regions.

Example:

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "mumbai"
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sydney"
  region = "ap-southeast-2"
}

Use provider alias:

resource "aws_instance" "sydney" {
  provider = aws.sydney

  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}
Important

Default provider:

provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

Aliased provider:

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sydney"
  region = "ap-southeast-2"
}
28. Terraform Workspaces

You practiced Terraform Workspaces as Day 5.

List:

terraform workspace list

Current workspace:

terraform workspace show

Create:

terraform workspace new dev

Create another:

terraform workspace new prod

Switch:

terraform workspace select dev

or:

terraform workspace select prod

Delete:

terraform workspace delete dev
29. terraform.workspace

Terraform exposes the current workspace through:

terraform.workspace

Example:

tags = {
  Name = "${terraform.workspace}-server"
}

In dev:

dev-server

In prod:

prod-server
30. Workspace Concept

Same Terraform configuration:

                Terraform Code
                     │
             ┌───────┴───────┐
             ↓               ↓
            dev             prod
             ↓               ↓
        dev state        prod state
             ↓               ↓
        Dev resources    Prod resources

Workspaces are useful when you want separate state for different environments using similar configuration.

31. Terraform Import

You practiced importing an existing EC2.

Import is used when:

AWS resource already exists
        ↓
Terraform doesn't manage it
        ↓
Import it
        ↓
Terraform manages it

Modern syntax:

import {
  id = "i-xxxxxxxx"
  to = aws_instance.example
}

And destination:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
}
32. Generate Configuration During Import

You practiced:

terraform plan -generate-config-out="generated.tf"

Terraform can generate a starting configuration based on the existing resource.

But always review generated configuration.

We encountered conflicting attributes such as:

primary_network_interface
vs
associate_public_ip_address

and:

ipv6_address_count
vs
ipv6_addresses

Generated code may need manual cleanup.

33. Import vs Configuration

Very important interview question.

Import

Brings the existing resource into:

Terraform State
Configuration generation

Helps create:

Terraform .tf configuration

Therefore:

Import is about bringing an existing resource under Terraform state management; generated configuration helps represent that resource in Terraform code.

34. Terraform Lifecycle

Terraform lifecycle meta-argument:

lifecycle {
  prevent_destroy = true
}

Other important options:

create_before_destroy = true

and:

ignore_changes = [
  tags
]
prevent_destroy

Prevents accidental destruction.

create_before_destroy

Creates the replacement before destroying the old resource when possible.

ignore_changes

Tells Terraform to ignore changes to specified attributes.

35. Terraform Data Sources

Data sources retrieve existing information instead of creating resources.

Example:

data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" {
  most_recent = true

  owners = ["amazon"]
}

Then:

ami = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id

Difference:

resource → creates/manages infrastructure

data → reads existing information
36. Terraform .terraform.lock.hcl

Terraform creates:

.terraform.lock.hcl

It records selected provider versions/checksums.

Example:

AWS provider
↓
version selection
↓
lock file

Unlike .terraform/ and state files, the lock file is normally committed to Git.

37. .terraform Directory

Terraform creates:

.terraform/

It contains Terraform working data and downloaded providers/modules.

Usually:

.terraform/

Do not commit it.

